From 3ed17ace659c31a627d9749707d46d2c64d86fcb Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
Message-Id: <mft-aac-configuration@streamary.invalid>
From: Streamary <noreply@streamary.invalid>
Date: Sat, 17 Oct 2026 20:20:49 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] mft: give Windows' AAC decoder the AudioSpecificConfig (HE-AAC)

Windows' AAC decoder reads its configuration from MF_MT_USER_DATA: the HEAACWAVEINFO fields that follow its
WAVEFORMATEX (12 bytes: payload type, profile and level, ...) and then the AudioSpecificConfig. The module took the
decoder's own input type, whose user data is those 12 bytes without a configuration, and kept it ("do not overwrite
existing user data"), so the stream's AudioSpecificConfig never reached the decoder. Without one the decoder takes the
declared sample rate as the AAC core rate and doubles it when it finds SBR: HE-AAC from MP4/MKV, whose container gives
the output rate (48 kHz over a 24 kHz core), came out at 96 kHz, switched mid-stream, and VLC played it cut, garbled
or silent. AAC-LC and HE-AAC from ADTS (whose header gives the core rate) were right by luck.

AAC now gets the 12 bytes (raw payload, profile not given) followed by the AudioSpecificConfig when there is one.
Checked with Tools/codec-probe/mfprobe (Streamary): explicit (2B118800) and implicit (1310) HE-AAC configurations
decode at 48 kHz with 2048 samples per frame whatever rate is declared; AAC-LC (1190) as before.

(libvlcjni checks this line: Message-Id: <mft-aac-configuration@streamary.invalid>)
---
 modules/codec/mft.c | 25 ++++++++++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 24 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/modules/codec/mft.c b/modules/codec/mft.c
index 9784f0e..8ba96f9 100644
--- a/modules/codec/mft.c
+++ b/modules/codec/mft.c
@@ -312,7 +312,30 @@ static int SetInputType(decoder_t *p_dec, DWORD stream_id, IMFMediaType **result
         }
     }
 
-    if (p_dec->fmt_in.i_extra > 0)
+    if (p_dec->fmt_in.i_codec == VLC_CODEC_MP4A)
+    {
+        /* Windows' AAC decoder reads its configuration from MF_MT_USER_DATA: the HEAACWAVEINFO fields after its
+         * WAVEFORMATEX (payload type, profile, ...; 12 bytes), then the AudioSpecificConfig. The decoder's own input
+         * type has those 12 bytes and no configuration, so it took the declared rate as the AAC core rate: HE-AAC
+         * (SBR) whose container gives the output rate (48 kHz over a 24 kHz core) came out at twice that rate, cut,
+         * garbled or silent. With the AudioSpecificConfig it decodes AAC-LC and HE-AAC (2048 samples per frame) at
+         * their real rate. Without one (raw frames from ADTS, whose header gave the core rate) it stays as it was. */
+        const size_t i_asc = p_dec->fmt_in.i_extra;
+        uint8_t *p_user_data = calloc(1, 12 + i_asc);
+        if (!p_user_data)
+            goto error;
+        p_user_data[0] = 0;    /* wPayloadType: raw AAC frames */
+        p_user_data[2] = 0xFE; /* wAudioProfileLevelIndication: not given (the configuration says it) */
+        if (i_asc)
+            memcpy(p_user_data + 12, p_dec->fmt_in.p_extra, i_asc);
+        hr = IMFMediaType_SetBlob(input_media_type, &MF_MT_USER_DATA, p_user_data, 12 + i_asc);
+        free(p_user_data);
+        if (FAILED(hr))
+            goto error;
+        msg_Dbg(p_dec, "AAC configuration: %zu bytes, %u Hz, %u channels declared", i_asc,
+                p_dec->fmt_in.audio.i_rate, p_dec->fmt_in.audio.i_channels);
+    }
+    else if (p_dec->fmt_in.i_extra > 0)
     {
         UINT32 blob_size = 0;
         hr = IMFMediaType_GetBlobSize(input_media_type, &MF_MT_USER_DATA, &blob_size);
-- 
